    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The parties who have been served in paragraph (2) must file and serve an affidavit in reply, if necessary"

-- "within 21 days after service": the anchor is the service of the originating application and affidavit.
-- src:2892 | (5) The parties who have been served in paragraph (2) must file and serve an affidavit in reply, if necessary, within 21 days after service.
@ref O 20 r 3(5) (src:2892-2892)
GIVEN `the date the affidavit in reply is filed` IS A DATE
      `the date the affidavit in reply is served` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 3(5) the affidavit in reply is filed and served in time` MEANS
    IF `the date the affidavit in reply is filed` AT MOST `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
       AND `the date the affidavit in reply is served` AT MOST `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "within 21 days after service" [period, ruled 2026-11-05 (a)]

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 4 -- case conference`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

`o 20 r 4(1) the period for the case conference` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 6
    `period unit` IS Weeks
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

-- "case conference" is an O 1 r 3(1) term (the keel's function): the meeting described in O 9 r 1, an input.
-- "attend" is also an O 1 r 3(1) term, but that definition "includes" electronic appearance and says nothing
-- of an ordinary appearance, so the keel's function answers NOTHING for the commonest case; the person's own
-- statement that the parties attended is therefore taken as the fact (NOTES.md, finding on `o 1 r 3(1) a person attends`).
-- src:2894 | 4. —(1) The parties must attend a case conference within 6 weeks after the originating application and the supporting affidavit are filed. (2) At the case conference, the Court must give the directions that are necessary for the appeal or the application.
@ref O 20 r 4(1) (src:2894-2894)
GIVEN `the parties attend the meeting` IS A BOOLEAN
      `o 9 r 1 the meeting is a case conference as described` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the date of the case conference` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 4(1) the parties attend a case conference in time` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the parties attend the meeting`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The parties must attend a case conference"
        IF `the date of the case conference` GREATER THAN `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "within 6 weeks after the originating application and the supporting affidavit are filed" [period, ruled 2026-11-05 (a)]
        OTHERWISE `o 1 r 3(1) a meeting is a case conference` `o 9 r 1 the meeting is a case conference as described`

-- (2): the Court "must give the directions that are necessary".  What it directs is the Court's; the
-- rule says only that directions are given.
-- src:2894 | 4. —(1) The parties must attend a case conference within 6 weeks after the originating application and the supporting affidavit are filed. (2) At the case conference, the Court must give the directions that are necessary for the appeal or the application.
@ref O 20 r 4(2) (src:2894-2894)
GIVEN `the Court is at the case conference` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 4(2) the Court must give the directions that are necessary` MEANS
    IF `the Court is at the case conference`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "At the case conference, the Court must give the directions that are necessary for the appeal or the application"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 5 -- written submissions`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

`o 20 r 5(1) the period for written submissions` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `Before the anchor`

-- "Subject to Rule 4": the directions given at the case conference may fix other times.  That is a fact the
-- person can state, and the rule itself names the exception, so it is an input.
-- src:2896 | 5. —(1) Subject to Rule 4, the parties must file and serve written submissions at least 14 days before the hearing of the appeal or the application. (2) The written submissions must be limited to 35 pages for each party or set of parties.
@ref O 20 r 5(1) (src:2896-2896)
GIVEN `the date the written submissions are filed` IS A DATE
      `the date the written submissions are served` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
      `the directions given at the case conference under Rule 4 fix a different time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 5(1) the written submissions are filed and served in time` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the directions given at the case conference under Rule 4 fix a different time`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Subject to Rule 4" and the Court's directions under Rule 4(2)
        IF `the date the written submissions are filed` AT MOST `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
           AND `the date the written submissions are served` AT MOST `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "at least 14 days before the hearing of the appeal or the application" [period, ruled 2026-11-05 (a)]

`o 20 r 5(2) the page limit for each party or set of parties` MEANS 35

-- src:2896 | 5. —(1) Subject to Rule 4, the parties must file and serve written submissions at least 14 days before the hearing of the appeal or the application. (2) The written submissions must be limited to 35 pages for each party or set of parties.
@ref O 20 r 5(2) (src:2896-2896)
GIVEN `the number of pages of the written submissions` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 5(2) the written submissions are within the page limit` MEANS
    IF `the number of pages of the written submissions` AT MOST `o 20 r 5(2) the page limit for each party or set of parties`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The written submissions must be limited to 35 pages for each party or set of parties."

-- (3): (b) "upon the payment of the fees and additional fees" counts as "where" (ruling of 2026-11-05 (c)):
-- unpaid fees is a "no".  (a) "in special circumstances" is a limit of the grant by its own wording: a
-- case without special circumstances is "not covered".  The fees are the Fourth Schedule's (an input).
-- src:2897 | (3) The Court may allow the page limit in paragraph (2) to be exceeded — ( a )
-- src:2898 | in special circumstances; and
-- src:2899 | ( b )
-- src:2900 | upon the payment of the fees and additional fees prescribed for the filing of pages in excess of the page limit.
@ref O 20 r 5(3) (src:2897-2900)
GIVEN `there are special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `sch 4 the fees and additional fees prescribed for the pages in excess of the limit have been paid` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 5(3) the Court may allow the page limit to be exceeded` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `sch 4 the fees and additional fees prescribed for the pages in excess of the limit have been paid`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "upon the payment of the fees and additional fees prescribed for the filing of pages in excess of the page limit" [upon counts as where: delegated-definitions ruling (c) of 2026-11-05]
        IF NOT `there are special circumstances`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in special circumstances"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 6 -- stay of enforcement`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- "does not operate as a stay ... unless any written law otherwise provides or the Court otherwise orders".
-- Both exceptions are the rule's own words, so both are facts the person can state.  When one holds, what
-- follows is the written law's or the Court's, which are not among the sources.
-- src:2902 | 6. An appeal or application does not operate as a stay of enforcement of the tribunal’s decision unless any written law otherwise provides or the Court otherwise orders. [S 206/2022 wef 01/04/2022]
@ref O 20 r 6 (src:2902-2902)
GIVEN `a written law provides that the appeal or application operates as a stay` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 6 the appeal or application operates as a stay of enforcement of the tribunal's decision` MEANS
    IF `a written law provides that the appeal or application operates as a stay` OR `the Court has ordered otherwise`
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "unless any written law otherwise provides or the Court otherwise orders"
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An appeal or application does not operate as a stay of enforcement of the tribunal’s decision"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 7 -- powers of the Court`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The powers are Order 19, Rule 7's (another part): the answer for a given power is an input, and this rule
-- adds only that the Court hearing an appeal or application under this Order has them.
-- src:2904 | 7. —(1) The Court hearing the appeal or the application has all the powers that the Court has under Order 19, Rule 7 when hearing an appeal after trial. (2) If an appeal to the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is permitted in the written law, the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal has all the powers that it has under Order 19, Rule 7 when hearing an appeal after trial.
@ref O 20 r 7(1) (src:2904-2904)
GIVEN `the Court is hearing the appeal or the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `o 19 r 7 the Court has the power when hearing an appeal after trial` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 7(1) the Court hearing the appeal or application has the power` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the Court is hearing the appeal or the application`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The Court hearing the appeal or the application"
        IF `o 19 r 7 the Court has the power when hearing an appeal after trial` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "all the powers that the Court has under Order 19, Rule 7" is Order 19, Rule 7's list

-- src:2904 | 7. —(1) The Court hearing the appeal or the application has all the powers that the Court has under Order 19, Rule 7 when hearing an appeal after trial. (2) If an appeal to the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is permitted in the written law, the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal has all the powers that it has under Order 19, Rule 7 when hearing an appeal after trial.
@ref O 20 r 7(2) (src:2904-2904)
GIVEN `an appeal to the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is permitted in the written law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is hearing it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `o 19 r 7 the Court has the power when hearing an appeal after trial` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 20 r 7(2) the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal has the power` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`an appeal to the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is permitted in the written law` AND `the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is hearing it`)
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If an appeal to the Appellate Division or Court of Appeal is permitted in the written law" [if]
        IF `o 19 r 7 the Court has the power when hearing an appeal after trial` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "all the powers that it has under Order 19, Rule 7" is Order 19, Rule 7's list
